course: dictionary
lesson: 372
title: Antiseptic: a word for germ-fighting
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An antiseptic is something you put on skin or a cut to kill germs and stop an infection starting. «Rinsing a graze under clean running water washes out grit; stinging antiseptic is not needed for most grazes.» So you can work out that a graze is a scrape on your skin, and grit is tiny bits of dirt.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. «When you graze your knee in the playground, clean running water washing out the grit does the main job.» The main job is cleaning, and germs are kept away from the cut. Antiseptic is one helper, but it is not always needed. Ask: what does the sentence say does the main job?
explain: Antiseptic can also describe a place. A hospital room can feel antiseptic, very clean but cold and without warmth. The word is used this way to describe how something looks or feels, not just what it does to germs. When you meet a word with more than one meaning, test each meaning in the sentence and choose the one that fits.
fact: An antiseptic is a substance that kills germs.
fact: You can use an antiseptic cream, wipe or spray on a cut or graze.
fact: «Antiseptic» is also an adjective: an antiseptic wipe is a wipe that kills germs.
fact: Clean running water washing out grit is often the most important first step for a graze.
fact: Joseph Lister began using antiseptics in surgery in the 1860s, and this saved many lives.
exercise: «Rinsing a graze under clean running water washes out grit; stinging antiseptic is not needed for most grazes.» What is an antiseptic? | A sticky plaster that keeps you warm | A type of grit from the playground | A stretchy bandage that keeps a wound still | *A substance that kills germs and stops infection
exercise: «When you graze your knee in the playground, clean running water washing out the grit does the main job.» Find and copy one word that means tiny bits of dirt or stone. | playground | *grit | graze | running
exercise: The nurse wiped the cut with an ____ wipe before she put on the plaster. Which word fits the gap? | *antiseptic | noisy | sticky | frozen
test: Which meaning of antiseptic is correct? | *Able to kill germs | Good at swimming in cold water | Full of sugar and sweets | Very loud and noisy
test: The hospital corridor was so antiseptic, white and cold, that it felt like nobody lived there. What does antiseptic mean here? | *Very clean but cold and without feeling | Full of toys and colours | Warm and cosy like a home | Dirty and smelly
exam: Why is stinging antiseptic not needed for most grazes? | *Clean running water washing out the grit does the main job | Because grit kills germs better than anything | Because antiseptic makes a graze bigger | Because grazes never need cleaning at all
